-- title:
--   Presentation-independence of square truncated presentations
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field and let $C$ be a commutative local $k$-algebra that is finite as a $k$-module. Fix $M \in \mathbb{N}$ and write, for $n \in \mathbb{N}$, $T_n$ for the truncated polynomial algebra $\mathrm{MvPolynomial}\,(\mathrm{Fin}\ n)\ k$ modulo the $(M+1)$st power of the kernel of the constant-coefficient homomorphism $\mathrm{MvPolynomial}\,(\mathrm{Fin}\ n)\ k \to k$ (that is, $k[X_0,\dots,X_{n-1}]/(X_0,\dots,X_{n-1})^{M+1}$). Suppose given a natural number $r$ and a surjective $k$-algebra homomorphism $\beta \colon T_r \to C$ whose kernel is generated by an $r$-indexed family $g\beta \colon \mathrm{Fin}\ r \to T_r$, i.e. $\ker \beta$ equals the ideal spanned by the range of $g\beta$; so $C$ admits one truncated presentation in which the number of relations matches the number of variables. Then for every natural number $N$ and every surjective $k$-algebra homomorphism $\alpha \colon T_N \to C$ there exists a family $h \colon \mathrm{Fin}\ N \to T_N$ with $\ker \alpha$ equal to the ideal spanned by the range of $h$: the kernel of any such presentation is generated by as many elements as there are variables.
--
--   This is the presentation-independence of the (zero-dimensional, truncated) complete-intersection property: if $C$ has one presentation over a truncated polynomial algebra with as many relations as variables, then every surjection from such an algebra onto $C$ has this property. It feeds the square-presentation statement [`Algebra.exists_ker_map_localization_eq_span_of_surjective_of_exists_square_presentation_of_surjective_algebraMap_residueField`](thm.html#Algebra.exists_ker_map_localization_eq_span_of_surjective_of_exists_square_presentation_of_surjective_algebraMap_residueField), part of the commutative-algebra input to the Taylor–Wiles patching argument.
-- source:
